::CW_U06_L01_Q01::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which portfolio selection reason is strongest?{
=A. I chose it because the revised dialogue shows clearer conflict between the characters.#Correct. This connects selection to craft growth. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. I chose it because it was first in my folder.#Incorrect. File order does not prove craft value.
~C. I chose it because it is short.#Incorrect. Length alone is not evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I chose it because I did not want to read another piece.#Incorrect. This is not a craft rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q02::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which item is evidence in a craft rationale?{
~A. A vague feeling that the piece is nice.#Incorrect. Evidence must be specific. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A random page number.#Incorrect. Page number alone is not craft evidence.
=C. A before-and-after revision showing a stronger final image.#Correct. This is specific evidence of growth. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. A statement that writing happened.#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q03::[MLA.CW.WR.08] A writer changed "I was afraid" to "I counted each floorboard between me and the door." What craft growth does this show?{
~A. Avoiding imagery.#Incorrect. The revision adds imagery. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Removing reader effect.#Incorrect. The revision strengthens reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Changing genre randomly.#Incorrect. The genre is not the issue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Showing emotion through action and image.#Correct. The revision turns direct emotion into a concrete moment.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q04::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which rationale is too vague?{
~A. The revised ending lets the reader infer the speaker's grief.#Incorrect. This is specific. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. This piece is good and I like it.#Correct. This gives opinion without evidence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. The dialogue revision clarifies conflict.#Incorrect. This names craft improvement. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The stanza break slows the final image.#Incorrect. This explains reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q05::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which three-part structure best fits a craft rationale?{
~A. Title, date, file size.#Incorrect. These do not explain craft. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Summary, summary, summary.#Incorrect. Summary alone is not a rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Selection claim, evidence, reader effect.#Correct. This structure explains why the piece belongs in the portfolio.
~D. Apology, complaint, unrelated detail.#Incorrect. This does not support selection. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q06::[MLA.CW.WR.08] A portfolio piece has strong imagery but no revision evidence. What should the writer add to the rationale?{
=A. A specific change, feedback note, or craft decision that proves growth.#Correct. This supplies evidence for the rationale. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. A random compliment.#Incorrect. The rationale needs evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A claim that evidence is unnecessary.#Incorrect. Evidence is required. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A statement about font color only.#Incorrect. Font color does not prove revision growth.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q07::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which sentence best explains reader effect?{
~A. I made changes.#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The piece has words.#Incorrect. This does not explain effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I wrote it last week.#Incorrect. Date written is not reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. The repeated image helps readers feel the speaker waiting even after the chance to leave passes.#Correct. This explains how the craft choice affects readers.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q08::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which selection should a writer question?{
~A. A piece that shows revision from telling to showing.#Incorrect. This may be a strong selection. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. A piece chosen only because it required no effort.#Correct. A portfolio piece should show craft value or growth.
~C. A piece with clear evidence of improved format.#Incorrect. This may support the portfolio. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A piece that demonstrates stronger dialogue after feedback.#Incorrect. This is a valid reason. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q09::[MLA.CW.WR.08] A craft rationale says, "I selected this because it is good." What is missing?{
~A. A longer title only.#Incorrect. The issue is missing evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A complaint about feedback.#Incorrect. That would not help. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Specific evidence and explanation of reader effect.#Correct. The claim needs support. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. A random summary.#Incorrect. Summary alone is not enough. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q10::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which answer best uses feedback evidence?{
~A. I ignored all feedback.#Incorrect. This does not use evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Feedback exists somewhere.#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I changed everything for no reason.#Incorrect. Revision should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Peer feedback showed the ending was too direct, so I revised the final sentence into an image that suggests hesitation.#Correct. This connects feedback, revision, and effect. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q11::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which is the best reason to include a weaker first draft excerpt in a rationale?{
=A. To show what changed and why the final version is stronger.#Correct. Before-and-after evidence can prove growth.
~B. To embarrass the writer.#Incorrect. The purpose is not shame. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. To replace the final piece.#Incorrect. It supports the final piece. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. To avoid explaining craft.#Incorrect. It should support craft explanation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q12::[MLA.CW.WR.08] What should a writer do before writing the final rationale?{
~A. Delete all revision notes.#Incorrect. Revision notes may be useful evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Decide evidence is unnecessary.#Incorrect. Evidence is required. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Choose evidence from the selected piece.#Correct. Evidence makes the rationale specific. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Write only "I like it."#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q13::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which piece best shows portfolio growth?{
~A. A rough draft with no changes.#Incorrect. It does not show growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. A poem revised from direct emotion into purposeful imagery after feedback.#Correct. This shows revision and craft growth. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. A piece selected only because it is short.#Incorrect. Length is not growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A story with no evidence or explanation.#Incorrect. The portfolio needs rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q14::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which statement best explains why reader effect matters?{
~A. Reader effect means readers are ignored.#Incorrect. It centers reader experience. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Reader effect is only spelling.#Incorrect. It is broader than conventions. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Reader effect means choosing randomly.#Incorrect. It requires purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Reader effect shows how a craft choice changes the audience's experience.#Correct. This connects writing decisions to impact. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q15::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which evidence best supports a rationale about dialogue growth?{
=A. The final draft includes clearer word choices for each character.#Correct. This is specific dialogue evidence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. The story has a title.#Incorrect. A title does not prove dialogue growth.
~C. The file is saved.#Incorrect. Saving a file is not craft evidence.
~D. The writer likes dialogue.#Incorrect. Preference is not evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q16::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which rationale is strongest?{
~A. It is nice.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. It is in my folder.#Incorrect. This does not explain craft value. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I selected this script because adding speaker labels and shorter stage directions made the conflict easier to perform and understand.#Correct. This names selection, evidence, and reader effect.
~D. I chose it randomly.#Incorrect. Random selection is weak. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q17::[MLA.CW.WR.08] A writer says, "The line breaks are better now." What should the writer add?{
~A. Nothing else.#Incorrect. The statement is incomplete. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Which line breaks changed and how they affect pacing or meaning.#Correct. Specific evidence and reader effect are needed.
~C. A random complaint.#Incorrect. That does not explain craft. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A denial that format matters.#Incorrect. Format can affect meaning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q18::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which answer best distinguishes summary from rationale?{
~A. Summary and rationale are always identical.#Incorrect. They serve different purposes. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Rationale never uses evidence.#Incorrect. Rationale requires evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Summary is always stronger than craft reasoning.#Incorrect. The portfolio requires rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Summary retells what happens; rationale explains why the piece was selected and what craft evidence supports it.#Correct. This separates content recap from craft reasoning.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q19::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which response best handles a piece the writer likes but cannot explain?{
=A. Look for specific craft evidence or choose another piece that better shows growth.#Correct. Portfolio choices must be supported. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Include it without evidence.#Incorrect. The rationale would be weak. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Delete the whole portfolio.#Incorrect. That is unnecessary. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Claim readers do not need reasons.#Incorrect. The assignment requires rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q20::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which phrase should be replaced in a craft rationale?{
~A. "The repeated image..."#Incorrect. This can introduce evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. "After feedback..."#Incorrect. This can introduce revision evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. "It is good."#Correct. This is vague and needs evidence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. "The reader can infer..."#Incorrect. This explains reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q21::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which is the best checkpoint submission?{
~A. A title only.#Incorrect. This is incomplete. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A list of favorite pieces with no explanation.#Incorrect. Portfolio selection needs rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A plot summary only.#Incorrect. Summary does not replace rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. A selected piece title, selection claim, two pieces of evidence, and reader-effect explanation.#Correct. This matches the lesson checkpoint. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q22::[MLA.CW.WR.08] What makes revision evidence stronger than general praise?{
~A. It avoids details.#Incorrect. Evidence needs details. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. It points to a specific change and explains why the final piece improved.#Correct. This proves growth. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. It says the piece is nice.#Incorrect. That is general praise. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It ignores reader effect.#Incorrect. Reader effect matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q23::[MLA.CW.WR.08] A rationale claims a story shows growth in structure. Which evidence best supports the claim?{
=A. The writer added a transition that clarifies the flashback.#Correct. This supports structural growth. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. The writer likes blue covers.#Incorrect. This is unrelated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The story was easy to find.#Incorrect. This is not structural evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The file name is short.#Incorrect. This does not show structure. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q24::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which answer shows weak selection thinking?{
~A. I selected this because feedback helped me revise the ending.#Incorrect. This is strong. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. I selected this because I did not want to think about another piece.#Correct. This is not a craft-based reason.
~C. I selected this because the imagery improved after revision.#Incorrect. This is strong. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I selected this because the final format supports the poem's pacing.#Incorrect. This is strong. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L01_Q25::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Which response best shows Lesson 1 mastery?{
~A. I can choose a piece because it is easy.#Incorrect. Ease is not a craft rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I can avoid evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is required. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I can choose a portfolio piece using craft evidence, revision evidence, and reader-effect explanation.#Correct. This summarizes the lesson skill. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. I can write only vague praise.#Incorrect. Vague praise is insufficient. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
